    {
      "id": "cycle-of-silence",
      "title": "Cycle Of Silence",
      "title_from_manifest": true,
      "group": "painting",
      "year": "2026",
      "medium": "Acrylic on wood",
      "edition": "1/1",
      "image": "https://assets.mintface.art/recent/cycle-of-silence.jpg",
      "aspect": 0.6663,
      "orientation": "portrait",
      "pricing_nzd": {
        "digital": null,
        "painting": 2500,
        "both": 2500
      },
      "added": "2026-08-17T00:01:00.000Z",
      "dimensions": {
        "w": 80,
        "h": 120,
        "d": 1,
        "unit": "cm"
      },
      "notes": null,
      "updated": "2026-08-17T23:53:11.231Z",
      "statement": "Read against Twombly, the work sits in that same territory where scrawl becomes elegy... the barbed wire strung like musical staves, the poppies bleeding down the white void, all held inside a vortex of red and yellow that spins like something you can't escape or stop repeating. The wire-as-notation is the sharpest move: silence literally scored, remembrance rendered as a song nobody sings. Where Twombly whispered classical myth through graphite, this shouts antipodean memory... Anzac poppies, rural fencing, the violence of enclosure... in a palette closer to Hundertwasser's spirals than Twombly's restraint.\n\nAgainst the MintFace canon it's a departure and a deepening. The strip paintings borrow Richter's cool, sampled logic... this is the opposite pole, hot and confessional, gesture over system. It suggests the canon has two engines: one that samples the world, and one that bleeds into it."
    },
